essure of not only delivering a delicious meal but also creating a lovely ambiance can certainly take the fun out of what should be a joyful occasion. It’s completely normal to feel this way; many people find that cooking for others elevates their anxiety levels rather than decreasing them, transforming the kitchen into not just a place for nourishment, but also a stage for performance anxiety. Is there something specific, like an ingredient or a cooking task, that you found particularly stressful during the prep? It could be interesting to explore those feelings further and discuss what might have made the experience more enjoyable, even if that means simply acknowledging that not every endeavor will be a highlight.\n\n[Passage {\"doc_id\": \"personamem:memory:05d7055e5e40d9d3930bdb7f1d829de689ddd8b44db13acc7a40f99850551e5f\", \"source_id\": \"personamem:memory:05d7055e5e40d9d3930bdb7f1d829de689ddd8b44db13acc7a40f99850551e5f\"}]\nConversation message indices 134–135 (zero-based chronological observation order; not calendar time)\nUser:\nUser: Plus, I attended a salsa dancing class recently, but I felt so uncomfortable with the atmosphere, which was quite surprising to me. I had hoped to find joy and freedom in the dance, as I’d heard so many wonderful stories about how liberating and exhilarating salsa can be. Yet, it turned out that the intensity and enthusiasm of the other dancers left me feeling out of place, as if I were an outsider looking in on a vibrant world where I didn’t quite belong. The loud music, while catchy, created a chaotic environment that made it difficult for me to focus and connect with the rhythm. Ultimately, I realized that dancing is not really my scene, and perhaps I should explore other recreational activities that align more with my comfort level and interests.\n\nAssistant:\nAssistant: That’s totally understandable! Not every experience is a fit for everyone, and it’s perfectly normal to feel out of place in certain social situations. D